# Clean Code: Unit Tests

- **Write automated tests, not throwaway drivers**, and check them in with production code.
- **The Three Laws of TDD:** (1) no production code until a failing test exists; (2) write only enough test to fail (not compiling = failing); (3) write only enough production code to pass the current test.
- **Keep tests as clean as production code.** Dirty tests get abandoned, and then the code rots. Tests preserve flexibility/maintainability by removing fear of change ("the -ilities").
- **Readability first.** Use a **BUILD-OPERATE-CHECK** structure and evolve a **domain-specific testing language** of helpers so tests say a lot with few expressions.
- **Dual standard:** test code may trade efficiency for simplicity (it runs in a test environment) — but never trade cleanliness.
- **One concept per test;** minimize asserts per test. Don't cram unrelated scenarios into one test function.

```java
// Clean, intent-revealing test via helper DSL
makePages("PageOne", "PageOne.ChildOne", "PageTwo");
submitRequest("root", "type:pages");
assertResponseIsXML();
assertResponseContains("<name>PageOne</name>");
```

## F.I.R.S.T
- **Fast** — slow tests don't get run.
- **Independent** — no test depends on another; any order.
- **Repeatable** — pass in any environment, offline included.
- **Self-Validating** — boolean pass/fail, no manual inspection.
- **Timely** — written just before the production code they cover.

## Heuristics (T1–T9)
- **T1** Tests are insufficient while any breakable condition is unverified.
- **T2** Use a coverage tool.
- **T3** Don't skip trivial tests.
- **T4** An ignored/commented test is a question about an ambiguity.
- **T5** Test boundary conditions.
- **T6** Bugs cluster — exhaustively test near a found bug.
- **T7** Patterns of failure are revealing.
- **T8** Coverage patterns of passing tests reveal why failures happen.
- **T9** Keep tests fast.
